  "pressure_reversal": {
   "note": "a few atmospheres antagonise anaesthesia (pressure reversal), so a krypton experiment at 2.5 atm cannot be compared with a xenon experiment at 1 atm. It can be compared with 84Kr at the same pressure: both arms sit at identical total pressure, so the reversal cancels in the isotope difference. The cost is that the absolute block is smaller than pressure-free potency predicts, which is why the block has to be measured in the chamber rather than assumed."
  },
  "nuclear_relaxation": {
   "note": "83Kr has I = 9/2 and a large quadrupole moment (0.259 b), so its nuclear relaxation in a cavity is fast by NMR standards - T1 of order 1-10 ms, i.e. 1e2-1e3 /s. That is 1e4-1e5 times slower than the radical pair recombines (5.5e7 /s), so the nuclear spin is static over the pair lifetime and the quadrupole cannot wash the effect out. The relaxation that matters is the electron's."
  }
